{
  "summary": "Round 9's synthesis is the most architecturally grounded proposal yet but has six unresolved issues: (1) the dependency_notes approach hijacks pipeline_log which has a fixed {ts, phase, output} schema consumed by _format_pipeline_log() and UI display layers — injecting a {type: 'dependency_rejected'} entry breaks that contract and forces fragile type-checking on log parsing; a dedicated dependency_events field or extending the existing plan_reviews pattern is cleaner; (2) the design treats dependency blocking as auto-run-only but doesn't address manual stage moves — _cmd_move_feature() currently allows moving any feature to any stage, so a user can manually push a blocked feature to 'approved' or 'implementing' via TUI/WebUI, bypassing the entire dependency system; this asymmetry (enforced for auto-mode, invisible for manual moves) must be an explicit design decision with either a warning, a confirmation prompt, or a hard block in the move handler; (3) computing the blocked field server-side during _broadcast_state() means building the full dependency graph on every mutation broadcast — since broadcasts fire on every edit (description change, title change, etc.), this is wasteful; the blocked status should be cached and only recomputed when a depends_on field or stage changes, not on every keystroke; (4) the DependencyGraph class in server.py and the 'simple standalone function' for CLI pipeline.py are two parallel implementations of the same resolution logic that must stay semantically identical — any future change to resolution semantics (e.g., adding a new terminal stage, changing how dangling references resolve) requires synchronized updates in both paths, which is a maintenance trap; both should share a single resolution function imported from state.py or a new deps.py module; (5) cycle detection in _cmd_edit_depends_on() has a TOCTOU gap — it reads the graph via list_all(), validates no cycle, then saves, but a concurrent edit to a different feature's depends_on could introduce a cycle between the check and the save; while rare, the auto-run loop should include a cycle-detection safety check as a backstop; (6) the discussion assumes ideation and planning can proceed without dependency awareness, but if feature B depends on feature A which is still in ideation, B's planning phase may produce plans assuming A's final design — the planning prompt should include dependency status context (which deps are resolved, which are still in-progress) so the LLM can hedge appropriately rather than building on assumptions about unfinished upstream features."
}
